 class="displayformula" style="max-height:100%;max-width:100%" border="0" width="160"
 height="37" alt="1/(2^s)+1/(3^s)+1/(5^s)+1/(7^s)+..." /></td><td align="right" width="10">
<div id="eqn2" class="eqnum">
(2)
</div>
</td></tr><tr style=""><td align="right" width=""><img src="/images/equations/PrimeNumber/Inline28.svg" class="displayformula" style="max-height:100%;max-width:100%" border="0" width="12"
 height="19" alt="" /></td><td align="center" width="14"><img src="/images/equations/PrimeNumber/Inline29.svg"
 class="displayformula" style="max-height:100%;max-width:100%" border="0" width="11"
 height="19" alt="=" /></td><td align="left"><img src="/images/equations/PrimeNumber/Inline30.svg"
 class="displayformula" style="max-height:100%;max-width:100%" border="0" width="36"
 height="19" alt="P(s)," /></td><td align="right" width="10">
<div id="eqn3" class="eqnum">
(3)
</div>
</td></tr>
</table>
</div>
<p>
where <img src="/images/equations/PrimeNumber/Inline31.svg" class="inlineformula" style="max-height:100%;max-width:100%" border="0" width="34" height="20" alt="P(s)" />
 is the <a href="/PrimeZetaFunction.html">prime zeta function</a> and <img src="/images/equations/PrimeNumber/Inline32.svg"
 class="inlineformula" style="max-height:100%;max-width:100%" border="0" width="22"
 height="20" alt="[S]" /> is an <a href="/IversonBracket.html">Iverson bracket</a>.
</p>
<p>
The function that gives the number of primes less than or equal to a number <img src="/images/equations/PrimeNumber/Inline33.svg" class="inlineformula" style="max-height:100%;max-width:100%"
 border="0" width="9" height="20" alt="n" /> is denoted <img src="/images/equations/PrimeNumber/Inline34.svg"
 class="inlineformula" style="max-height:100%;max-width:100%" border="0" width="33"
 height="20" alt="pi(n)" /> and is called the <a href="/PrimeCountingFunction.html">prime
 counting function</a>. The theorem giving an asymptotic form for <img src="/images/equations/PrimeNumber/Inline35.svg"
 class="inlineformula" style="max-height:100%;max-width:100%" border="0" width="33"
 height="20" alt="pi(n)" /> is called the <a href="/PrimeNumberTheorem.html">prime
 number theorem</a>. Similarly, the numbers of primes of the form <img src="/images/equations/PrimeNumber/Inline36.svg"
 class="inlineformula" style="max-height:100%;max-width:100%" border="0" width="49"
 height="20" alt="ak+b" /> less than or equal to a number <img src="/images/equations/PrimeNumber/Inline37.svg"
 class="inlineformula" style="max-height:100%;max-width:100%" border="0" width="9"
 height="20" alt="n" /> is denoted <img src="/images/equations/PrimeNumber/Inline38.svg"
 class="inlineformula" style="max-height:100%;max-width:100%" border="0" width="49"
 height="22" alt="pi_(a,b)(n)" /> and is called the <a href="/ModularPrimeCountingFunction.html">modular
 prime counting function</a>.
</p>
<p>
<img src="/images/equations/PrimeNumber/Inline39.svg" class="inlineformula" style="max-height:100%;max-width:100%" border="0" width="33" height="20" alt="pi(n)" /> and <img src="/images/equations/PrimeNumber/Inline40.svg"
 class="inlineformula" style="max-height:100%;max-width:100%" border="0" width="18"
 height="20" alt="p_n" /> are inverse functions, so
</p>
<div>
<table summary="" width="100%" align="center" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="0" style="padding-left: 50px">
<tr><td align="left"><img src="/images/equations/PrimeNumber/NumberedEquation1.svg" class="numberedequation" style="max-height:100%;max-width:100%" border="0" width="69"
 height="19" alt=" pi(p_n)=n " /></td><td align="right" width="3">
<div id="eqn4" class="eqnum">
(4)
</div>
</td></tr>
</table>
</div>
<p>
for all positive integers and
</p>
<div>
<table summary="" width="100%" align="center" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="0" style="padding-left: 50px">
<tr><td align="left"><img src="/images/equations/PrimeNumber/NumberedEquation2.svg" class="numberedequation" style="max-height:100%;max-width:100%" border="0" width="62"
 height="20" alt=" p_(pi(n))=n " /></td><td align="right" width="3">
<div id="eqn5" class="eqnum">
(5)
</div>
</td></tr>
</table>
</div>
<p>
<a href="/Iff.html">iff</a> <img src="/images/equations/PrimeNumber/Inline41.svg" class="inlineformula" style="max-height:100%;max-width:100%" border="0" width="9"
 height="20" alt="n" /> is a prime number.
</p>
<p>
Many <a href="/PrimeFactorizationAlgorithms.html">prime factorization algorithms</a> have been devised for determining the <a href="/PrimeFactor.html">prime factors</a>
 of a given <a href="/Integer.html">integer</a>, a process known as factorization
 or prime factorization. They vary quite a bit in sophistication and complexity. It
 is <i>very</i> difficult to build a general-purpose algorithm for this computationally
 &quot;hard&quot; problem, so any additional information which is known about the
 number in question or its factors can often be used to save a large amount of time.
 It should be emphasized that although no efficient algorithms are known for factoring
 arbitrary integers, it has not been <i>proved</i> that no such algorithm exists.
 It is therefore conceivable that a suitably clever person could devise a general
 method of factoring which would render the vast majority of encryption schemes in
 current widespread use, including those used by banks and governments, easily breakable.
</p>
<p>
Because of their importance in encryption algorithms such as <a href="/RSAEncryption.html">RSA encryption</a>, prime numbers can be important commercial commodities. In fact, R.&nbsp;Schlafly
 (1994) has obtained U.S. Patent <img src="/images/equations/PrimeNumber/Inline42.svg"
 class="inlineformula" style="max-height:100%;max-width:100%" border="0" width="69"
 height="20" alt="5373560" /> on the following two primes (expressed in <a href="/Hexadecimal.html">hexadecimal</a>
 notation):
</p>
<div>
<table summary="" width="100%" align="center" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="0" style="padding-left: 50px">
<tr><td align="left"><img src="/images/equations/PrimeNumber/NumberedEquation3.svg" data-src-small="/images/equations/PrimeNumber/NumberedEquation3_400.svg" data-src-default="/images/equations/PrimeNumber/NumberedEquation3.svg" class="numberedequation swappable" style="max-height:100%;max-width:100%" width="435" data-big="435 81" data-small="389 209"
