<div id="eqn7" class="eqnum">
(7)
</div>
</td></tr>
</table>
</div>
<p>
The <a href="/FundamentalTheoremofArithmetic.html">fundamental theorem of arithmetic</a> states that any <a href="/PositiveInteger.html">positive integer</a> can be represented
 in <a href="/ExactlyOne.html">exactly one</a> way as a <a href="/Product.html">product</a>
 of primes. <a href="/EuclidsTheorems.html">Euclid's second theorem</a> demonstrated
 that there are an infinite number of primes. However, it is not known if there are
 an infinite number of primes <a href="/OftheForm.html">of the form</a> <img src="/images/equations/PrimeNumber/Inline43.svg"
 class="inlineformula" style="max-height:100%;max-width:100%" border="0" width="43"
 height="20" alt="n^2+1" /> (Hardy and Wright 1979, p.&nbsp;19; Ribenboim 1996, pp.&nbsp;206-208),
 whether there are an <a href="/Infinite.html">infinite</a> number of <a href="/TwinPrimes.html">twin
 primes</a> (the <a href="/TwinPrimeConjecture.html">twin prime conjecture</a>), or
 if a prime can always be found between <img src="/images/equations/PrimeNumber/Inline44.svg"
 class="inlineformula" style="max-height:100%;max-width:100%" border="0" width="16"
 height="20" alt="n^2" /> and <img src="/images/equations/PrimeNumber/Inline45.svg"
 class="inlineformula" style="max-height:100%;max-width:100%" border="0" width="54"
 height="20" alt="(n+1)^2" /> (Hardy and Wright 1979, p.&nbsp;415; Ribenboim 1996,
 pp.&nbsp;397-398). The latter two of these are two of <a href="/LandausProblems.html">Landau's
 problems</a>.
</p>
<p>
The simplest method of finding factors is so-called &quot;<a href="/DirectSearchFactorization.html">direct search factorization</a>&quot; (a.k.a. <a href="/TrialDivision.html">trial division</a>).
 In this method, all possible factors are systematically tested using trial division
 to see if they actually <a href="/Divide.html">divide</a> the given number. It is
 practical only for very small numbers. More general (and complicated) methods include
 the <a href="/EllipticCurveFactorizationMethod.html">elliptic curve factorization
 method</a> and <a href="/NumberFieldSieve.html">number field sieve</a> factorization
 method.
</p>
<p>
It has been proven that the set of prime numbers is a <a href="/DiophantineSet.html">Diophantine
set</a> (Ribenboim 1991, pp.&nbsp;106-107).
</p>
<p>
With the exception of 2 and 3, all primes are of the form <img src="/images/equations/PrimeNumber/Inline46.svg" class="inlineformula" style="max-height:100%;max-width:100%" border="0" width="82"
 height="20" alt="p=6n+/-1" />, i.e., <img src="/images/equations/PrimeNumber/Inline47.svg"
 class="inlineformula" style="max-height:100%;max-width:100%" border="0" width="118"
 height="20" alt="p=1,5 (mod 6)" /> (Bungus 1599, p.&nbsp;399, quoted in Peano 1908,
 p.&nbsp;59; Wells 1986, p.&nbsp;68). For <img src="/images/equations/PrimeNumber/Inline48.svg"
 class="inlineformula" style="max-height:100%;max-width:100%" border="0" width="9"
 height="20" alt="n" /> an <a href="/Integer.html">integer</a> <img src="/images/equations/PrimeNumber/Inline49.svg"
 class="inlineformula" style="max-height:100%;max-width:100%" border="0" width="29"
 height="20" alt="&gt;=2" />, <img src="/images/equations/PrimeNumber/Inline50.svg"
 class="inlineformula" style="max-height:100%;max-width:100%" border="0" width="9"
 height="20" alt="n" /> is prime <a href="/Iff.html">iff</a> the <a href="/CongruenceEquation.html">congruence
 equation</a>
</p>
<div>
<table summary="" width="100%" align="center" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="0" style="padding-left: 50px">
<tr><td align="left"><img src="/images/equations/PrimeNumber/NumberedEquation5.svg" class="numberedequation" style="max-height:100%;max-width:100%" border="0" width="165"
 height="38" alt=" (n-1; k)=(-1)^k (mod n) " /></td><td align="right" width="3">
<div id="eqn8" class="eqnum">
(8)
</div>
</td></tr>
</table>
</div>
<p>
holds for <img src="/images/equations/PrimeNumber/Inline51.svg" class="inlineformula" style="max-height:100%;max-width:100%" border="0" width="41" height="20" alt="k=0" />,
 1, ..., <img src="/images/equations/PrimeNumber/Inline52.svg" class="inlineformula"
 style="max-height:100%;max-width:100%" border="0" width="37" height="20" alt="n-1" />
 (Deutsch 1996), where <img src="/images/equations/PrimeNumber/Inline53.svg" class="inlineformula"
 style="max-height:100%;max-width:100%" border="0" width="30" height="40" alt="(n; k)" />
 is a <a href="/BinomialCoefficient.html">binomial coefficient</a>. In addition, an
 integer <img src="/images/equations/PrimeNumber/Inline54.svg" class="inlineformula"
 style="max-height:100%;max-width:100%" border="0" width="9" height="20" alt="n" />
 is prime <a href="/Iff.html">iff</a>
</p>
<div>
<table summary="" width="100%" align="center" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="0" style="padding-left: 50px">
<tr><td align="left"><img src="/images/equations/PrimeNumber/NumberedEquation6.svg" class="numberedequation" style="max-height:100%;max-width:100%" border="0" width="127"
 height="19" alt=" phi(n)+sigma(n)=2n. " /></td><td align="right" width="3">
<div id="eqn9" class="eqnum">
(9)
</div>
</td></tr>
</table>
</div>
<p>
The first few composite <img src="/images/equations/PrimeNumber/Inline55.svg" class="inlineformula" style="max-height:100%;max-width:100%" border="0" width="9" height="20" alt="n" />
 for which <img src="/images/equations/PrimeNumber/Inline56.svg" class="inlineformula"
 style="max-height:100%;max-width:100%" border="0" width="120" height="21" alt="n|[phi(n)+sigma(n)]" />
 are <img src="/images/equations/PrimeNumber/Inline57.svg" class="inlineformula" style="max-height:100%;max-width:100%"
 border="0" width="59" height="20" alt="n=312" />, 560, 588, 1400, 23760, ... (OEIS
 <a href="http://oeis.org/A011774">A011774</a>; Guy 1997), with a total of 18 such
 numbers less than <img src="/images/equations/PrimeNumber/Inline58.svg" class="inlineformula"
 style="max-height:100%;max-width:100%" border="0" width="51" height="20" alt="2&#215;10^7" />.
</p>
<p>
Chen (1979) showed that for <img src="/images/equations/PrimeNumber/Inline59.svg" class="inlineformula" style="max-height:100%;max-width:100%" border="0" width="9"
 height="20" alt="x" /> sufficiently large, there always exists a number with at least
 two prime factors between <img src="/images/equations/PrimeNumber/Inline60.svg" class="inlineformula"
 style="max-height:100%;max-width:100%" border="0" width="43" height="20" alt="x-x^alpha" />
 and <img src="/images/equations/PrimeNumber/Inline61.svg" class="inlineformula" style="max-height:100%;max-width:100%"
 border="0" width="9" height="20" alt="x" /> for <img src="/images/equations/PrimeNumber/Inline62.svg"
 class="inlineformula" style="max-height:100%;max-width:100%" border="0" width="91"
 height="20" alt="alpha&gt;=0.477..." /> (Le Lionnais 1983, p.&nbsp;26; Guy 2004,
 p.&nbsp;34). In practice, this relation seems to hold for all <img src="/images/equations/PrimeNumber/Inline63.svg"
 class="inlineformula" style="max-height:100%;max-width:100%" border="0" width="65"
 height="20" alt="x&gt;2521" />.
</p>
<p>
Primes consisting of consecutive <a href="/Digit.html">digits</a> (counting 0 as coming after 9) include 2, 3, 5, 7, 23, 67, 89, 4567, 78901, ... (OEIS <a href="http://oeis.org/A006510">A006510</a>).
 Primes consisting of digits that are themselves primes include 23, 37, 53, 73, 223,
 227, 233, 257, 277, 337, 353, 373, 523, 557, ... (OEIS <a href="http://oeis.org/A019546">A019546</a>),
 which is one of the <a href="/SmarandacheSequences.html">Smarandache sequences</a>.
</p>
<p>
Because a prime number <img src="/images/equations/PrimeNumber/Inline64.svg" class="inlineformula" style="max-height:100%;max-width:100%" border="0" width="11" height="20" alt="p" />
 has only the <a href="/Trivial.html">trivial</a> factors 1 and <img src="/images/equations/PrimeNumber/Inline65.svg"
 class="inlineformula" style="max-height:100%;max-width:100%" border="0" width="11"
 height="20" alt="p" />, in his <i><a href="http://www.amazon.com/exec/obidos/ASIN/0670772895/ref=nosim/ericstreasuretro">The
 Road Ahead</a></i>, Bill Gates accidentally referred to a trivial operation when
 he stated &quot;Because both the system's privacy and the security of digital money
 depend on encryption, a breakthrough in mathematics or computer science that defeats
 the cryptographic system could be a disaster. The obvious mathematical breakthrough
 would be the development of an easy way to <i>factor large prime numbers</i> [emphasis
 added]&quot; (Gates 1995, p.&nbsp;265).
</p>
</div>
